# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is gitignored — keep it that way.
#
# Find these at https://sanity.io/manage -> your project.

# Not secret. It appears in every cdn.sanity.io image URL the site serves.
SANITY_PROJECT_ID=
SANITY_DATASET=production

# SECRET. Optional: only needed to preview UNPUBLISHED content under
# `astro dev`. A production build works without it and sees published
# documents only.
#
# Create it under API -> Tokens with VIEWER permissions. Viewer is enough to
# read drafts and cannot write anything, so a leak of this token cannot alter
# the site. Never use an Editor or Deploy token here.
SANITY_READ_TOKEN=

# Optional. Where the Studio's "Preview" pane points — a hosted build made
# with `npm run build:preview`, which shows unpublished work. Leave it unset
# and the Preview tab is not registered at all, which is better than one that
# shows a broken frame. See README.
# SANITY_STUDIO_PREVIEW_URL=https://preview.aniwala.com

# The Studio (studio/) reads these two. Same values, different names — the
# SANITY_STUDIO_ prefix is what the Sanity CLI exposes to the Studio bundle.
SANITY_STUDIO_PROJECT_ID=
SANITY_STUDIO_DATASET=production

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supabase — where the booking, application and comment forms post.
#
# Until BOTH are set, all three forms refuse to submit and say so rather
# than dropping data into a void. Create a free project at supabase.com,
# run ALL of `supabase/schema.sql` in its SQL editor (that creates the
# tables AND the Row Level Security policies protecting them), then copy
# these from Project Settings -> API.
#
# The anon key is PUBLIC by design — it ships in the JavaScript bundle and
# RLS is what protects the data. It lives here because it is configuration,
# not because it is a secret. NEVER put the `service_role` key here: it
# bypasses RLS entirely and this value is compiled into a public website.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
SUPABASE_URL=
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cloudflare Turnstile — the "are you human" check on the three forms.
#
# BUILD TIME, and PUBLIC like the anon key: it identifies the widget to
# Cloudflare and has to be in the page for the widget to render. Its
# partner, TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY, is set on the Edge Functions with
# `supabase secrets set` and must NEVER appear in this file — it is what
# proves a token is genuine.
#
# OPTIONAL, and leaving it blank is a supported state. Blank means the
# forms post straight to PostgREST under the anon key exactly as they
# always did, protected by RLS and the database rate limiter. Fill it in
# and they switch to posting through the `submit` Edge Function, which
# verifies the token before writing anything.
#
# Get one at dash.cloudflare.com -> Turnstile -> Add site. Add the
# SAME value as a GitHub Actions repository secret, or production
# builds will keep taking the fallback path while your local one does
# not — the failure mode nobody notices.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Email notifications — Supabase Edge Functions + Resend
#
# Not read by the website build. These are consumed by
# `supabase secrets set` and by the Supabase CLI. Optional: skip the
# whole block and nothing breaks — enquiries, applications and
# comments still land in Supabase and you read them in the dashboard.
# What you lose is being told about them.
#
# Full setup is in README.md under "Getting it all by email".
# ------------------------------------------------------------------

# resend.com -> API Keys.
RESEND_API_KEY=

# Deploy-time only. supabase.com/dashboard/account/tokens
# ACCOUNT-WIDE — it reaches every project you own. Revoke it once the
# functions are deployed.
SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# Must be on a domain verified in Resend. Before you have one, use
# Aniwala <onboarding@resend.dev> — it delivers only to the address
# owning the Resend account, which is enough to test the chain.
MAIL_FROM=

# Where enquiries and bookings go, and where job applications go
# instead. Separate on purpose: a CV filed in among the client briefs
# is a CV that gets missed.
MAIL_DEFAULT=
MAIL_CAREERS=

# Optional per-service routing. Unmatched falls through to
# MAIL_DEFAULT, so leaving it unset is a valid choice.
# MAIL_ROUTES={"Animation":"art@aniwala.com"}

# SECRETS. Generate your own, one each:  openssl rand -base64 36
#
# MODERATION_SECRET signs the Approve / Reject links in comment
# emails — an HMAC over the row id AND the action, so an approve link
# cannot be edited into a reject one or replayed on another comment.
#
# NOTIFY_SECRET is checked on every call to the notify function.
# Without it, anyone who found the URL could post fake enquiries.
MODERATION_SECRET=
NOTIFY_SECRET=

# Signs the Confirm / Cannot-make-it links on a call request, the
# same way. OPTIONAL: unset, the schedule function uses
# MODERATION_SECRET instead, which is safe because the signature
# covers the row id and the action — a comment's approve token is not
# a booking's confirm token. Set it if you would rather the two
# rotate independently.
# BOOKING_SECRET=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Booking confirmations — all optional, all cosmetic except the first
# ------------------------------------------------------------------

# NEITHER OF THESE IS NEEDED FOR A CALL TO HAVE SOMEWHERE TO HAPPEN.
# Every booking already gets a room of its own, derived from its id —
# pre-filled on the confirmation screen, stable if you confirm twice,
# and never shared with another booking. These two only change where
# that room lives, or replace it.

# A room for EVERY call, instead of one per booking. A Zoom personal
# meeting ID belongs here if you would rather use it: it has a waiting
# room, which is what makes one shared room safe. A bare Google Meet
# link does not — two bookings an hour apart would share it, and an
# overrunning call would put one client inside another's.
# MEETING_URL=https://zoom.us/j/0000000000

# Where the generated rooms live. Any service whose rooms are just a
# name in a URL works — a self-hosted Jitsi, a Whereby team subdomain.
# Defaults to the public Jitsi instance, which needs no account.
# MEETING_ROOM_BASE=https://meet.jit.si

# The name on the invitation and in the subject line. Defaults to
# "Aniwala Studios".
# STUDIO_NAME=Aniwala Studios

# YOUR timezone, printed beside the visitor's on every booking email
# so nobody diaries a client's local time by mistake. Defaults to
# Asia/Kolkata. Display only — the instant itself is stored in UTC.
# STUDIO_TZ=Asia/Kolkata

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Studio mirror — every submission copied into Sanity
#
# Optional. With a write token set, the notify function also writes
# each booking, brief, application and comment into Sanity as a
# read-only "Form submission" document, so the whole intake can be
# read in the Studio instead of the Supabase dashboard. Supabase stays
# the source of truth. Leave the token unset and nothing is copied.
#
# A SUPABASE SECRET, not a build variable:
#   supabase secrets set SANITY_WRITE_TOKEN=sk...
#
# SANITY_PROJECT_ID and SANITY_DATASET are at the top of this file for
# the website build; the function needs its own copies in the Supabase
# environment under the same names.
#
# THE TOKEN IS NOT SCOPED TO A DOCUMENT TYPE. Anything holding it can
# rewrite or delete every document in the dataset — which is exactly
# why this write happens in an Edge Function and never in the browser.
# Create one at sanity.io/manage -> API -> Tokens, with Editor rights.
# SANITY_WRITE_TOKEN=

SITE_URL=

# SUPABASE_URL and SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY are deliberately absent:
# Supabase injects both into the Edge Function environment itself. The
# service key bypasses RLS entirely and must never enter this repo.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# DEPLOY CREDENTIALS ARE NOT HERE, and that is deliberate.
#
# The deploy runs in GitHub Actions, never on your machine, so its
# credentials live only in the repository secrets:
#
#   SSH_HOST  SSH_USER  SSH_PORT  SSH_KEY
#
# A copy in `.env` would be a copy that can leak from a laptop while
# doing nothing useful — nothing in this repo reads them.
#
# OBSOLETE: if your `.env` still has FTP_HOST, FTP_USER or FTP_PASS,
# delete them. The deploy moved from FTP to SSH and nothing reads
# those any more. Rotate the FTP password too if it was ever used —
# deploys before `protocol: ftps` sent it in the clear.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
